00:02:03.320 --> 00:02:10.999
So you’re a Johnny Cash fan, aren’t we
all, well he stole Folsom Prison Blues almost

00:02:10.999 --> 00:02:16.909
word-for-word from a song released two years
earlier by another American composer: Gordon

00:02:16.909 --> 00:02:21.859
Jenkins, his song was called Crescent City
Blues and if you listen to it, in another

00:02:21.859 --> 00:02:26.299
tab, of course, then you’ll notice that
the melody and more than half the words are

00:02:26.299 --> 00:02:29.779
identical to Folsom Prison Blues.

00:02:29.779 --> 00:02:35.989
Cash tried to get away with his forgery too,
he didn’t credit Jenkins anywhere on his

00:02:35.989 --> 00:02:39.909
record which sold many thousands more copies
than the original.

00:02:39.909 --> 00:02:46.500
Cash was later sued after his version became
a worldwide hit, the lawsuit cost him $75,000.

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:54.739
Van Gogh’s 30 masterpieces were all precise
replicas of 30 artworks by other artists.

00:02:54.739 --> 00:03:00.279
Although I am being slightly unfair on the
mad Dutchman, he actually admitted that these

00:03:00.279 --> 00:03:09.180
were all copies at the time, in fact, that
was his intention to copy, as closely as possible,

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:10.549
works that he admired.

00:03:10.549 --> 00:03:15.290
Though it’s still interesting to learn that
even a master such as Van Gogh needed to copy

00:03:15.290 --> 00:03:17.290
now and then for inspiration.

00:03:17.290 --> 00:03:21.209
And Shakespeare, well he stole a lot of his
work.

00:03:21.209 --> 00:03:26.529
Othello was a story borrowed from a previous
Italian novelist Giovanni Cinthio.

00:03:26.529 --> 00:03:31.670
Hamlet was stolen from 12th-century Danish
writer Saxo Grammaticus.

00:03:31.670 --> 00:03:38.089
And Romeo and Juliet, yes that was stolen
too, from a poem written by Arthur Brooke

00:03:38.089 --> 00:03:45.529
in 1562, the poem was even called ‘The Tragicall
Historye of Romeus and Juliet’.

00:03:45.529 --> 00:03:52.379
But am I angry that these legends of the arts,
the titans of creativity have been lying to

00:03:52.379 --> 00:03:53.379
us?

00:03:53.379 --> 00:03:58.859
Hell no, stealing other people’s work was
the best thing they ever did.

00:03:58.859 --> 00:04:03.790
These people may have nicked their treasures
but who cares when they did such wonderful

00:04:03.790 --> 00:04:05.819
things with the booty.

00:04:05.819 --> 00:04:12.109
Crescent City Blues sounds great but Folsom
Prison Blues sold better because let’s be

00:04:12.109 --> 00:04:16.690
honest, it’s a hell of a lot catchier and
has a higher production value than the original

00:04:16.690 --> 00:04:17.940
song.

00:04:17.940 --> 00:04:22.620
Shakespeare arguably did greater things with
the material he copied than anyone else in

00:04:22.620 --> 00:04:23.660
history.

00:04:23.660 --> 00:04:29.120
He transformed the stories he stole into truly
beautiful creations that elevated the English

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:35.920
language to a godly level, permanently enriching
the tapestry of mankind in the process.

00:04:35.920 --> 00:04:40.350
The fact that Shakespeare stole these stories
doesn’t disparage his work in the slightest,

00:04:40.350 --> 00:04:45.320
he was still a literary genius and potentially
no one else could have given these existing

00:04:45.320 --> 00:04:49.270
tales such a breath of brilliant new life.

00:04:49.270 --> 00:04:53.560
If he had copied the stories word for word
then that’s a whole different ball game

00:04:53.560 --> 00:04:56.030
and that’s not ok.

00:04:56.030 --> 00:05:05.180
But truthfully, copyright theft doesn’t
matter, it’s what you do with it that counts.

00:05:05.180 --> 00:05:08.931
These three examples are just the tip of the
iceberg when it comes to famous works that

00:05:08.931 --> 00:05:10.980
are actually copies.

00:05:10.980 --> 00:05:16.640
In fact, I would argue that the majority of
all creative works you know today, books,

00:05:16.640 --> 00:05:21.180
music, film etc. are copied from someone who
came earlier.

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:27.410
If this is true then it begs the question:
Is any idea truly original?

00:05:27.410 --> 00:05:35.280
Well, no; there was a point in history when
truly original ideas were still possible,

00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:40.400
but such a rare phenomenon has not existed
for at least 2,000 years - and probably not

00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:44.830
since humans began building large, connected
civilisations.

00:05:44.830 --> 00:05:50.270
Your brain is a collection of millions of
memories collected over your entire life.

00:05:50.270 --> 00:05:55.610
Whenever you have an idea you aren’t always
aware but you subconsciously borrow little

00:05:55.610 --> 00:06:00.800
bits of these memories and combine them to
imagine something new.

00:06:00.800 --> 00:06:06.470
You could derive this new idea from bits of
hundreds of different memories or just three,

00:06:06.470 --> 00:06:11.750
but you can’t avoid it, this is how the
human brain works.

00:06:11.750 --> 00:06:19.360
The inception of a 100% unique idea is not
possible because the brain always has to draw

00:06:19.360 --> 00:06:21.990
on prior knowledge.

00:06:21.990 --> 00:06:27.300
To frustrate matters even further in most
creative fields there are actually a very

00:06:27.300 --> 00:06:33.860
limited amount of possibilities that can be
realised, which makes repeats or at least

00:06:33.860 --> 00:06:37.980
very close duplicates inevitable.

00:06:37.980 --> 00:06:40.320
Take for example a novel.

00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:46.200
You would think there are limitless possibilities
for writing fiction, but not quite.

00:06:46.200 --> 00:06:49.020
At least that’s not what Christopher Booker
thought.

00:06:49.020 --> 00:06:56.470
He postulated that there are only seven possible
plots and all works of fiction ever written

00:06:56.470 --> 00:06:58.660
fall into one of them.

00:06:58.660 --> 00:06:59.660
They are:

00:06:59.660 --> 00:07:00.950
Overcoming the monster.

00:07:00.950 --> 00:07:04.530
Think Beowulf, War of the Worlds, James Bond.

00:07:04.530 --> 00:07:06.480
Rags to riches.

00:07:06.480 --> 00:07:09.960
Cinderella, Jane Eyre, Great Expectations.

00:07:09.960 --> 00:07:11.050
The quest.

00:07:11.050 --> 00:07:14.480
The Lord of The Rings, The Pilgrim’s Progress.

00:07:14.480 --> 00:07:16.430
Voyage and return.

00:07:16.430 --> 00:07:20.880
Alice in Wonderland, Peter Rabbit, Gone With
The Wind.

00:07:20.880 --> 00:07:21.890
Comedy.

00:07:21.890 --> 00:07:24.830
Much Ado About Nothing, Twelfth Night.

00:07:24.830 --> 00:07:25.830
Tragedy.

00:07:25.830 --> 00:07:29.020
Macbeth, Romeo and Juliet.

00:07:29.020 --> 00:07:30.270
Rebirth.

00:07:30.270 --> 00:07:33.500
A Christmas Carol, Beauty and The Beast.

00:07:33.500 --> 00:07:39.020
You can go round and round in your mind all
day conjuring up wild storylines that may

00:07:39.020 --> 00:07:41.490
not fit into one of these seven.

00:07:41.490 --> 00:07:48.820
And if you do you’ll probably end up at
some truly stupid destinations: such as the

00:07:48.820 --> 00:07:55.200
tale of a man who grows a third nipple and
has to take up underwater basket weaving to

00:07:55.200 --> 00:07:57.520
make enough money to have it removed.

00:07:57.520 --> 00:08:03.450
But then you realise that’s just an f’in
bizarre example of ‘The Quest’ or possibly

00:08:03.450 --> 00:08:07.390
‘Overcoming the monster’ depending on
how you look at it.

00:08:07.390 --> 00:08:12.310
And even within these categories, you often
find that so many acclaimed stories are the

00:08:12.310 --> 00:08:13.310
same.

00:08:13.310 --> 00:08:18.320
Within the human experience, there are only
finite things that a character in a book can

00:08:18.320 --> 00:08:20.140
do.

00:08:20.140 --> 00:08:25.350
Because of this, every now and then a very
odd phenomenon occurs, films are released

00:08:25.350 --> 00:08:32.630
by completely unrelated and independent studios
that have identical plots, and neither was

00:08:32.630 --> 00:08:36.769
aware of the other film’s existence until
their release.

00:08:36.769 --> 00:08:41.900
This is so eerily common that it has been
given a name: ‘Twin Films’.

00:08:41.900 --> 00:08:42.900
For example:

00:08:42.900 --> 00:08:49.300
In 1986 Top Gun was released and so was a
remarkably similar but spectacularly less

00:08:49.300 --> 00:08:51.959
successful film called Iron Eagle.

00:08:51.959 --> 00:08:56.509
In 1998 there was Antz and A Bug’s Life.

00:08:56.509 --> 00:09:01.410
In 1999 there was The Mummy and Tale of The
Mummy.

00:09:01.410 --> 00:09:06.910
In 2006 there was The Illusionist and The
Prestige.

00:09:06.910 --> 00:09:09.649
These are just a few examples of many.

00:09:09.649 --> 00:09:14.430
Some twin films have been attributed to espionage
within the film industry, but many others

00:09:14.430 --> 00:09:18.800
are pure coincidences, after all over 2,500
films are released each year.

00:09:18.800 --> 00:09:24.589
But it could also be due to the fact that
there are only a limited amount of possible

00:09:24.589 --> 00:09:30.579
storylines, that can be altered, sure, but
the core story remains the same.

00:09:30.579 --> 00:09:35.990
Even when you are absolutely certain that
your new idea is entirely original you may

00:09:35.990 --> 00:09:39.709
actually be suffering from a case of ‘cryptomnesia’.

00:09:39.709 --> 00:09:46.029
This is when you recall a memory you had forgotten
and you mistakenly think its an original idea

00:09:46.029 --> 00:09:48.259
that you have just generated.

00:09:48.259 --> 00:09:55.259
But in reality the joke, or concept for a
book, film etc. is something you heard of

00:09:55.259 --> 00:10:01.740
saw a long time ago and had just buried deep
in the recesses of your mind.

00:10:01.740 --> 00:10:09.839
As you can imagine cryptomnesia can get you
in trouble if you are adamant that your idea

00:10:09.839 --> 00:10:17.300
is original but it turns out to be plagiarised,
and cryptomnesia has come for many well-known

00:10:17.300 --> 00:10:18.300
people.

00:10:18.300 --> 00:10:23.019
When the American writer Helen Keller was
11 she wrote a short story called ‘The Frost

00:10:23.019 --> 00:10:24.019
King’.

00:10:24.019 --> 00:10:28.110
But later in her life, it was discovered that
the entire story was plagiarised from a fairytale

00:10:28.110 --> 00:10:30.199
called Frost Fairies.

00:10:30.199 --> 00:10:35.160
Helen Keller had no idea she was committing
plagiarism at the time, she was suffering

00:10:35.160 --> 00:10:42.230
from cryptomnesia but she later recalled that
she had, in fact, read Frost Faries four years

00:10:42.230 --> 00:10:45.050
before she wrote The Frost King.

00:10:45.050 --> 00:10:49.540
She got into trouble and her name was dragged
through the mud by the papers.

00:10:49.540 --> 00:10:55.100
The event left Keller a nervous wreck and
she would never write fiction again.

00:10:55.100 --> 00:11:01.300
So if having an original idea is almost impossible
and if you do then it’s probably just cryptomnesia

00:11:01.300 --> 00:11:06.709
anyway, then it begs the question: should
we all be committing plagiarism and just hoping

00:11:06.709 --> 00:11:10.490
to get away with it or should we go the other
way and all put paper bags over our heads

00:11:10.490 --> 00:11:14.730
and sit in the corner shaking, because we’re
afraid that if we utter a single word we could

00:11:14.730 --> 00:11:16.240
be breaking the law?

00:11:16.240 --> 00:11:22.750
Well, there is actually a happy medium and
it’s what 99% of us do when we want to create

00:11:22.750 --> 00:11:27.019
a great piece of honest creative work.

00:11:27.019 --> 00:11:29.949
Plagiarism is obviously morally wrong.

00:11:29.949 --> 00:11:33.879
Plagiarism is taking someone else’s work
or concept and passing it off as your own

00:11:33.879 --> 00:11:36.339
with little to no changes to its content.

00:11:36.339 --> 00:11:43.980
I would personally abhor anyone who practices
plagiarism and hopes to get away with it.

00:11:43.980 --> 00:11:49.110
But there's isn't anything inherently wrong
with using one or more pieces of other people's

00:11:49.110 --> 00:11:55.600
work as heavy inspiration, so long as you
add value.

00:11:55.600 --> 00:12:01.470
When true originality isn't possible, and
it seldom is, then greatness can only be achieved

00:12:01.470 --> 00:12:03.980
by adding value.

00:12:03.980 --> 00:12:09.790
Few of Shakespeare's works are genuinely original
but he achieved greatness time after time

00:12:09.790 --> 00:12:16.589
by taking already successful stories and rewriting
them using astonishing language to elevate

00:12:16.589 --> 00:12:22.110
them to a new level that the original writer
could never reach.

00:12:22.110 --> 00:12:26.420
Imagine if Shakespeare had to come up with
a truly original storyline each time he wrote

00:12:26.420 --> 00:12:32.829
a play, he would have far less time to focus
on carefully crafting beautiful lines and

00:12:32.829 --> 00:12:39.399
probably wouldn’t have written nearly as
many plays for future generations to enjoy.

00:12:39.399 --> 00:12:44.870
Using other people’s work allows one to
not get caught in a hopeless black hole of

00:12:44.870 --> 00:12:50.579
imagination, trying to fathom some immaculate
conception of an original idea that will likely

00:12:50.579 --> 00:12:52.019
never come.

00:12:52.019 --> 00:12:57.540
By bypassing this step and building upon the
work of others you can get a head start and

00:12:57.540 --> 00:13:04.940
focus on adding significant value to truly
make it into a unique piece that may actually

00:13:04.940 --> 00:13:17.740
improve upon the original.

00:13:17.740 --> 00:13:22.330
Forget about whether what you are working
on has been done a hundred times before and

00:13:22.330 --> 00:13:29.339
focus on what you personally can add to it
that nobody else has, do this authentically

00:13:29.339 --> 00:13:33.980
and with passion, and you will create something
amazing.

00:13:33.980 --> 00:13:40.079
In doing so you will not be alone, the best
musicians of the past century have taken inspiration

00:13:40.079 --> 00:13:45.110
from the great classical composers that laid
the groundwork for them.

00:13:45.110 --> 00:13:52.110
And what is Star Wars if not an amalgamation
of spaghetti westerns, samurai films and footage

00:13:52.110 --> 00:13:56.559
of Spitfire dogfights during World War II,
all set in space?

00:13:56.559 --> 00:14:02.540
Instead of being intimidated by all the great
work that has come before you, let it instead

00:14:02.540 --> 00:14:07.939
inspire you and there is no limit to what
you could achieve.

00:14:07.939 --> 00:14:09.610
Thanks for watching.

